│ The root cause of the issue is that the user is attempting to      │
│ override get_FIELD_display on the model itself, which is not the   │
│ correct approach in Django. Instead, the method should be handled  │
│ via the Meta class's display_field attribute. Here's the           │
│ breakdown:                                                         │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                          Key Observations                          │
│                                                                    │
│  1 Incorrect Method Override:                                      │
│    The user's code attempts to override get_foo_bar_display on the │
│    model, but Django does not expose this method directly in the   │
│    model class. Instead, it is handled via the Meta class's        │
│    display_field attribute.                                        │
│  2 Django 2.2+ Behavior:                                           │
│    In Django 2.2+, the display_field attribute is used to specify  │
│    the field to display, and the get_FIELD_display method is not   │
│    directly called on the model. This is a change in how Django    │
│    handles field display logic.                                    │
│  3 Correct Approach:                                               │
│    To override the display behavior, the display_field should be   │
│    set in the Meta class of the model. This ensures the model's    │
│    get_FIELD_display method is called with the correct field name. │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                              Solution                              │
│                                                                    │
│ Update the FooBar model to use the Meta class's display_field      │
│ attribute:                                                         │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  class FooBar(models.Model):                                       │
│      foo_bar = models.CharField(_("foo"), choices=[(1, 'foo'), (2  │
│  'bar')])                                                          │
│      class Meta:                                                   │
│          display_field = 'foo_bar'                                 │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ This ensures the get_FIELD_display method is called with the       │
│ correct field name, and the display value is returned as expected. │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                   Why the Initial Search Failed                    │
│                                                                    │
│ The initial search for get_FIELD_display in the Django codebase    │
│ did not find the method, which is expected because it is not       │
│ directly exposed in the model class. Instead, it is handled via    │
│ the Meta class, confirming the need to adjust the code to use      │
│ display_field instead.                                             │
