│                       🔍 Analysis of Context                       │
│                                                                    │
│ The issue revolves around the behavior of Django's __str__ method  │
│ in Django 2.2+, where the model's __str__ method calls the         │
│ display() method of the field, not get_FIELD_display(). This       │
│ change in behavior is not documented in the Django changelog, and  │
│ it is not supported in Django 2.2+.                                │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                        🧠 Key Observations                         │
│                                                                    │
│  1 Model Class Not Found:                                          │
│     • The search API search_class_in_file("FooBar", "models.py")   │
│       returned no results, indicating the class is not in          │
│       models.py. This suggests the class is in a different file or │
│       module.                                                      │
│  2 __str__ Method Calls display():                                 │
│     • The __str__ method of the model calls                        │
│       self.foo_bar.display(), not get_FIELD_display(). This        │
│       confirms that the display() method is the correct path for   │
│       overriding field display logic in Django 2.2+.               │
│  3 Expected Behavior:                                              │
│     • The get_FIELD_display() method is not available in the       │
│       context of the model's __str__ method in Django 2.2+. The    │
│       correct approach is to use the field's display() method.     │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                             🛠️ Solution                             │
│                                                                    │
│ The correct approach is to use the field's display() method        │
│ instead of overriding get_FIELD_display(). This is the only valid  │
│ way to override field display logic in Django 2.2+.                │
│                                                                    │
│                              Example:                              │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│  class FooBar(models.Model):                                       │
│      foo_bar = models.CharField(_("foo"), choices=[(1, 'foo'), (2  │
│  'bar')])                                                          │
│                                                                    │
│      def __str__(self):                                            │
│          return self.foo_bar.display()                             │
│                                                                    │
│                                                                    │
│ ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── │
│                           📁 Next Steps                            │
